# Cascade — The table

This file defines the geometry of the table: the size of a card, where each of
the thirteen piles sits, how the cards of a pile are laid out, and the rectangle
each pile answers a release in. What each pile accepts is in
`specs/foundations.md`, `specs/tableau.md`, and `specs/stock.md`; the gestures
resolved against these rectangles are in `specs/controls.md`. Every position is a
top-left corner in the stage's logical units, as `specs/overview.md` states.

## A card

Every card occupies a `CARD_W x CARD_H` (`100 x 140`) rectangle. That is its
footprint wherever it sits: in a pile, overlapped in a column, held in hand, or
in flight during the victory cascade. A card is drawn face-up, showing its rank
and its suit, or face-down, showing its back.

## The order of a pile

A pile is a stack ordered from the bottom card to the top card. The top card of
the stock, the waste, or a foundation is the one drawn over the rest of that
pile. A tableau column is fanned downward instead, so its top card is the one
drawn **lowest** on the table, and that card is the column's exposed card: the
one a run is stacked onto and the one a move takes first.

## The piles

The table holds thirteen piles in two rows. The top row carries the stock, the
waste, and the four foundations; the seven tableau columns run beneath it.

### Column anchors

The seven columns are evenly spaced, at a pitch of `122`, which is a `100`-wide
card and a `22` gap. `COLUMN_X` holds their left edges:

| Column | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6 |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `COLUMN_X` | `224` | `346` | `468` | `590` | `712` | `834` | `956` |

The gaps between the columns carry no pile and nothing card-sized is drawn in
them.

### The top row

The top row sits at `TOP_ROW_Y` (`24`) and holds six piles, each anchored on one
of the column positions above:

| Pile | Anchor |
| --- | --- |
| The stock | `(STOCK_X, TOP_ROW_Y)`, `(224, 24)` |
| The waste | `(WASTE_X, TOP_ROW_Y)`, `(346, 24)` |
| Foundation `0` | `(FOUNDATION_X[0], TOP_ROW_Y)`, `(590, 24)` |
| Foundation `1` | `(FOUNDATION_X[1], TOP_ROW_Y)`, `(712, 24)` |
| Foundation `2` | `(FOUNDATION_X[2], TOP_ROW_Y)`, `(834, 24)` |
| Foundation `3` | `(FOUNDATION_X[3], TOP_ROW_Y)`, `(956, 24)` |

`FOUNDATION_X` is `[590, 712, 834, 956]`. The third column position, `x = 468`,
carries no pile in the top row and nothing card-sized is drawn there. It is the
space that separates the two draw piles on the left from the foundations on the
right.

The stock and the foundations are squared piles: every card sits at the pile's
anchor, so the pile shows its top card alone.

### The waste
{{#if (eq variant.slug "draw-three")}}

The cards the waste shows, which `specs/stock.md` defines, are fanned to the
right from the waste anchor at a pitch of `WASTE_FAN` (`26`), oldest first, so
with three cards shown they are drawn with their top-left corners at
`(346, 24)`, `(372, 24)`, and `(398, 24)`, and the last of them is the waste's
top card. At most three cards fan, so the fan begins at `346` and its right edge
never passes `498`. Every other card the waste holds is squared away at the
waste anchor beneath them.
{{else}}

The card the waste shows, which `specs/stock.md` defines, is drawn with its
top-left at the waste anchor, and every other card the waste holds is squared
away beneath it.
{{/if}}

A waste whose set memory is empty shows no card, so it draws the empty-slot mark
at its anchor whatever cards it still holds.

### Empty piles

A pile holding no cards draws a card-sized mark at its anchor, `CARD_W x CARD_H`,
so an empty slot reads apart from the bare table.

### The columns

Each column begins at `TABLEAU_Y` (`180`), at its own `COLUMN_X`. Its cards fan
downward from there, each one offset below the card above it by:

| The card above is | Offset |
| --- | --- |
| Face-down | `FACE_DOWN_OFFSET` (`24`) |
| Face-up | `FACE_UP_OFFSET` (`34`) |

So a column's first card has its top edge at `y = 180`, and each further card's
top edge is that many units below the one before it.

### Long-column compression

A column's lowest card's bottom edge may not fall below `COLUMN_BOTTOM_LIMIT`
(`676`). When a column's natural extent at the offsets above would pass that
line, its face-up offset is reduced uniformly, the same value under every face-up
card of that column, to the largest value that fits the column above the line.
The reduced offset never falls below `FACE_UP_OFFSET_MIN` (`14`), and a column
long enough to demand less than `14` draws `14`. The face-down offset stays at
`24` however far a column is compressed.

The fit is made per column and per frame, so a column that compressed and then
lost cards draws the full `34` offset again.

## Drop rectangles

Each pile answers a release inside one rectangle, and `specs/controls.md` states
how a release resolves against them.

| Pile | Its drop rectangle |
| --- | --- |
| The stock | `CARD_W x CARD_H` at `(STOCK_X, TOP_ROW_Y)` |
| The waste | `CARD_W x CARD_H` at `(WASTE_X, TOP_ROW_Y)`, whatever the shown set fans to |
| A foundation | `CARD_W x CARD_H` at `(FOUNDATION_X[i], TOP_ROW_Y)` |
| An empty column | `CARD_W x CARD_H` at `(COLUMN_X[i], TABLEAU_Y)` |
| A column holding cards | `CARD_W` wide at `COLUMN_X[i]`, running from `TABLEAU_Y` down to the bottom edge of that column's lowest drawn card |

The top row's rectangles end at `y = 164` and the columns' begin at `y = 180`, so
no two of the thirteen rectangles overlap and a point lies in at most one of
them. A point in none of them lies on no pile.

## The HUD strip

The band `STAGE_W` wide at `y = HUD_Y` (`680`), `HUD_H` (`36`) tall, is the HUD
strip. It carries the three controls and the deal-mode label `specs/screens.md`
defines, and it holds no pile. `COLUMN_BOTTOM_LIMIT` keeps every column clear of
it.
